According to PSL, "The judiciary also has recognized that punishments
imposed for student conduct _off school grounds_ must be supported by
evidence that the student behavior outside of school has a detrimental
impact on the well-being of other pupils, teachers, or school activities.
[Omitted--discussion of upheld sanctions for fighting, insulting a teacher,
and making offensive remarks about a teacher outside of school.]  Courts,
however, have prohibited school authorities from punishing students for
misbehavior off school grounds if pupils have not been informed that such
conduct would result in sanctions [20] or if the misbehavior has had no
direct relationship to the welfare of the school. [21]"

[20] See Galveston Independent School Dist. v. Boothe, 590 S.W.2d 553
(Tex. Civ. App. 1979).

[21] See Thomas v. Board of Educ., Grantville Cent. School Dist., 607 F.2d
1043 (2d Cir. 1979), cert. denied, 444 U.S. 1081 (1980); Klein v. Smith,
635 F.Supp. 1440 (D. Me. 1986).


It would seem clear that, as I said before, the mere possession and showing
of a pocket knife outside of school does not affect the welfare of the
school, and as such cannot be regulated by the school.  (It also seems very
unlikely that the school would have announced a rule prohibiting such.)

Ostensibly, they could argue that he brought the knife to school--but
considering that he didn't use or display it, possibly didn't even realize
it the entire time he was at school, and certainly didn't intend to bring
it, 10 days of suspension (suspension at all, in fact) would seem to be an
unjustifiably severe punishment.

And certainly, if they claim to be punishing him on that basis, they need
to make that clear.


