And I appear to be correct.  According to PSL, "In the absence of greater
specificity in state statutes or administrative regulations, students facing
short-term suspensions have a constitutional right to the following
protections prior to suspension:

  "- oral or written notification of the nature of the violation and the
     intended punishment;
  "- an opportunity to refute the charges before an objective decision maker
     (such a discussion may immediately follow the alleged rule infraction);
     and
  "- an explanation of the evidence upon which the disciplinarian is relying."


[...]


In addition, PSL discusses unconstitutionally vague regulations:

"Policies prohibiting 'improper conduct' and behavior 'inimical to the best
interests of the school' have been invalidated because they have not specified
the nature of the impermissible conduct. [9]  Courts have recognized, however,
that disciplinary regulations do not have to satisfy the stringent criteria
or specificity require in criminal statutes. [10]  Less detail has been
required in regulations dealing with activites that are clearly disruptive,
such as behavior interfering with the right of access to school buildings. [11]"

[9] See Mitchell v. King, 363 A.2d 68 (Conn. 1975); Soglin v. Kauffman, 418
F.2d 163 (7th Cir. 1969).

[10] Bethel School Dist. No. 403 v. Fraser, 106 S.Ct. 3159 (1986).

[11] See Sill v. Pennsylvania State Univ., 462 F.2d 463 (3d Cir. 1972).


The most recent post from Dave Witte states that

>They now claim that he "materially and substantially disrupted the 
>rights of those other students to receive an education".  When asked 
>in writing to provide supportive evidence, they've not done so.

It seems clear that none of the allegations the school has made qualify as
materially or substantially disruptive.  They are no doubt trying to work
around the fact that they have *no* rule which covers the situation at
hand.

What's more, the refusal to provide supporting evidence is a clear violation
of the child's due process rights.


For Dave's benefit, PSL provides the following relevant information regarding
remedies for unlawful disciplinary actions:

"For unwarranted suspensions or expulsions, students are entitled to
reinstatement without penalty to grades and to have their school records
expunged of any reference to the illegal disciplinary action. [164]"

and

"In _Wood v. Strickland_, the Supreme Court stated that school officials
could be held liable for monetary damages under the Civil Rights Act of 1871
if they arbitrarily violated students' federally protected rights in
disciplinary proceedings. [167]  The Court declared that ignorance of the
law could not be used as a valid defense to shield school officials if they
should have known that their actions would impair "clearly established"
federal rights of students.  Under the _Wood_ proclamations, a showing of
malice is not always required in order to prove that the actions of school
officials were taken in bad faith.  However, a mere mistake in carrying out
duties does not render school authorities liable."

[164] See, e.g., McEntire v. Brevard County School Bd., 471 So. 2d 1287 (Fla.
App. 1985); Quinlan v. University Place School Dist., 660 P.2d 329 (Wash.
App. 1983); John A. v. San Bernardino City Unified School Dist., 654 P.2d
242 (Cal. 1982).

[167] 420 U.S. 308 (1975).  Section 1983 of the Civil Rights Act of 1871, 42
U.S.C. $ 1983, provides a damages remedy for deprivations of federally
protected rights under color of state law.  [Forward reference to a note
about Section 1983 creating personal liability omitted...]


For Dave Witte...

I suspect that it's fairly unlikely that you're going to get rich off a
suit stemming from this action.  However, you might well be able to use
the threat of financial liability (and, in particular, the potential for
*personal* liability) to get this matter resolved fairly--which I'd expect
includes an admission of error by the school, expunging the records and an
appropriate apology.

I take their latest claim (re: "materially and substantially disrupted") to
be evidence of cognizance of their awkward legal position.  If you pounce
now, with credible case and statute citations, I suspect there's a good
chance that they'll back down entirely, in exchange for an agreement not
to sue.


In any case, I think the excerpts I've provided validate the basic claims I
made earlier regarding the legitimacy of the school's actions (as they have
been described to us).



